<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Lab 4: Systems of Differential Equations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p component="prod-02"><term>Due: Tuesday, November 24</term></p>
    <p component="prod-04"><term>Due: Monday, November 23</term></p>

    <p>
      <alert>Names:</alert>
    </p>
    
    <p>
      <term>Instructions:</term> The exercises here should be
      completed in groups of 2 or 3 students with one write-up
      submitted from each group.  Be sure to include everyone's name
      on the write-up.
    </p>

    <p>
      Up to this point, we have looked at
      differential equations that describe a single
      function.  It sometimes happens, however,
      that we find two quantities whose rates of
      change depend on one another.  In this case,
      we have a system of differential equations.
    </p>
    <p>
      In today's lab, we'll begin by looking at a
      system of differential equations that can be
      used to describe the interaction of two
      different species, one of whom preys on the
      other.  For instance, we will consider a
      population of wolves <m>W(t)</m> that prey
      upon a population of deer <m>D(t)</m>.
      Let's build two differential equations that
      describe how the populations change.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        First of all, we will suppose that if there
        were no wolves, the deer would increase at a
        rate proportional to their population:
        <md>
          \frac{dD}{dt} = 0.08D
          \quad\text{or}\quad
          \frac{dD/dt}{D} = 0.08
        </md>
        where time <m>t</m> is measured in days.
        In other words, the per capita growth
        rate is constant.
      </p>
      <p>
        If there are now some wolves around, we would
        expect that the per capita growth rate would
        decrease.  For instance, we might find that
        <md>
          \frac{dD/dt}{D} = (0.08 - 0.001W)
          \quad\text{or}\quad
          \frac{dD}{dt} = (0.08 - 0.001W)D.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        In the same way, we will suppose that if
        there were no deer, the wolves would die off,
        since they have no food, at a rate
        proportional to their population:
        <md>
          \frac{dW}{dt} = -0.02W.
        </md>
        However, if there are some deer around, we
        expect the wolves' per capita growth rate to
        increase.  For instance,
        <md>
          \frac{dW/dt}{W} = (-0.02 + 0.00002D)
          \quad\text{or}\quad
          \frac{dW}{dt} = (-0.02 + 0.00002D)W.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The pair of equations
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \frac{dD}{dt}
            \amp= (0.08 - 0.001W)D
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \frac{dW}{dt}
            \amp= (-0.02 + 0.00002D)W
          </mrow>
        </md>
        is called the <em>Lotka-Volterra model</em> for
        predator-prey interaction.  Though a simple
        model, the basic effects predicted by the
        model have been observed in nature.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose at some time, there are 1500 deer
          and 60 wolves.  Is the deer population
          increasing or decreasing?  Is the wolf
          population increasing or decreasing?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For what values of <m>W</m> does the deer
          population increase?  For what values of
          <m>D</m> does the wolf population
          increase?  Does this agree with your
          intuition about the situation?  Why or
          why not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Are there values for <m>D</m> and
          <m>W</m> so that neither quantity changes?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose initially there are <m>1000</m>
          deer and <m>40</m> wolves; that is,
          <m>D(0) = 1000</m> and <m>W(0) = 40</m>.
          Use Excel to implement Euler's method
          with a step size of <m>h = 0.1</m> to
          find out how the populations behave over
          the course of one year.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your spreadsheet will probably begin
          something like this:
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>h</m></cell>
            <cell><m>t</m></cell>
            <cell><m>D</m></cell>
            <cell><m>W</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\Delta D</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\Delta W</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1</cell>
            <cell>0.0</cell>
            <cell>1000.0</cell>
            <cell>40.0</cell>
            <cell>4.00</cell>
            <cell>0.00</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1</cell>
            <cell>0.1</cell>
            <cell>1004.0</cell>
            <cell>40.0</cell>
            <cell>4.02</cell>
            <cell>0.00</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1</cell>
            <cell>0.2</cell>
            <cell>1008.0</cell>
            <cell>40.0</cell>
            <cell>4.03</cell>
            <cell>0.00</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1</cell>
            <cell>0.3</cell>
            <cell>1012.0</cell>
            <cell>40.0</cell>
            <cell>4.05</cell>
            <cell>0.00</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Sketch a graph of the populations
          <m>D(t)</m> and <m>W(t)</m> over one year
          below and discuss any interesting features
          that you see.  In particular, you should
          discuss the general behavior of the two
          graphs along with any relationships you
          notice between the two graphs.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        We'll now examine another system of
        differential equations, this time imagining
        two species <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> that
        compete for food.  If there is one species
        without the other, we will assume a
        logistic model for population growth; say,
        <md>
          \frac{dx}{dt} = x(1-x)
        </md>
        where <m>x</m> is measured in millions.
        However, if there is some of the species
        <m>y</m> around, it will lower the growth
        rate of <m>x</m> to, say,
        <md>
          \frac{dx}{dt} = x(1-x-y).
        </md>
        Species <m>y</m> behaves in the same way:
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dt} = y(0.75-y-0.5x)
        </md>
        so we have the system of equations
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \frac{dx}{dt}
            \amp= x(1-x-y)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \frac{dy}{dt}
            \amp= y(0.75-y-0.5x).
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Set up a spreadsheet to study this system
          of equations with the initial values
          <m>x(0) = 1.5</m> and <m>y(0) = 0.25</m>
          using a step size of <m>h = 0.1</m>.
          Make a plot of <m>x(t)</m> and
          <m>y(t)</m>.  What happens after a very
          long time?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Study what happens with a few different
          initial values.  What do you notice?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If the constants are adjusted just a
          little bit, there is a very different kind
          of behavior.  Consider the system of
          equations
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dx}{dt}
              \amp= x(1-x-y)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dy}{dt}
              \amp= y(0.5-0.25y-0.75x).
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Study this system with <m>x(0) = 1.5</m>
          and <m>y(0) = 0.25</m>.  Make a plot of
          <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m>.  What
          happens after a very long time?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now consider the initial values
          <m>x(0) = 0.5</m> and <m>y(0) = 1</m>.
          What happens after a very long time?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How do these two examples of systems of
          competing species differ from one another?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
